{"step_index":1168,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T11:14:59Z","content":"Yes, significant improvement. Here's the before vs after:\n\n### ✅ What Improved\n\n| Area | Before (Overfitted) | After (Calibrated) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Loss** | 0.0032 ☠️ (memorized) | **0.82** ✅ (generalized) |\n| **Identity** | Working | Working ✅ |\n| **Vuln Detection** (SQLi) | Working but brittle | Working ✅ — CWE-89, CVSS 9.8 |\n| **Training Data** | 0 benign samples | **108 balanced** (45 vuln + 35 benign + 28 identity) |\n| **Generalization** | Poor — model memorized patterns | **Much better** — smooth learning curve |\n| **Train Time** | 5m 26s | **82 seconds** (4× faster) |\n\n### ⚠️ What Still Needs Work\n\n| Issue | Status |\n|---|---|\n| **False positives** on safe parameterized SQL (`%s` binding) | Still triggers — TEST 3 fails |\n\nThe model no longer overfits, correctly identifies real vulnerabilities, and maintains its Fluxnat identity. The one remaining gap is **benign discrimination** — it's too aggressive and flags safe `cursor.execute(query, (user_id,))` patterns as SQLi.\n\n**To fix that**, the next training round would need more benign SQL samples with explicit `\"Status: SECURE\"` labels so the model learns `%s` parameter binding ≠ string interpolation.\n\nWant me to prepare an improved dataset to fix the false-positive issue?","thinking":"The user is asking \"is there any improvement?\" - they want to know if the calibrated retraining actually improved the model compared to the overfitted version. Let me think about what improvements were made and summarize them clearly.\n\nFrom the context, I can compare:\n\n**Before (Overfitted Run):**\n- 60 steps, 5 epochs, loss collapsed to 0.0032\n- r=64, α=128\n- 0 benign samples\n- Model was severely overfitting - memorizing training data\n\n**After (Calibrated Run):**\n- 25 steps, 2 epochs, final loss 0.82\n- r=16, α=32, LR=1e-4, with 108 balanced samples across vuln, benign, and identity categories - much healthier convergence\n\nLooking at test results, identity check passes with correct self-identification, and CWE-89 SQL injection detection correctly flags the vulnerability with proper CVSS scoring. The benign parameterized query test appears to have failed though, showing a false positive.\n\nThere's real progress: healthy loss (0.82 vs the previous 0.003 overfit), correct vulnerability detection still working, identity intact, and training running 4x faster. But the false positive issue on safe parameterized SQL remains unresolved - worth flagging clearly when I present these findings."}
{"step_index":1169,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T11:16:16Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nwhat does it mean \n\nFalse positives on safe parameterized SQL (%s binding)\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-22T16:46:16+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":1170,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T11:16:21Z","content":"**False positive** = the model says \"DANGER!\" when there's actually no danger.\n\nHere's the difference:\n\n### ❌ Actual SQL Injection (Dangerous)\n```python\nquery = f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '{user_input}'\"\ncursor.execute(query)\n```\nA hacker can type `' OR 1=1 --` and steal your entire database. **This IS dangerous** — the model should flag it. ✅\n\n### ✅ Parameterized SQL (Safe)\n```python\nquery = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = %s\"\ncursor.execute(query, (user_input,))\n```\nThe `%s` is a **placeholder** — the database engine handles the user input separately, so hackers can't inject anything. **This is the safe, correct way to write SQL.** The model should say \"all good.\" \n\n### The Problem\n\nOur model flags **both** as dangerous. It sees `%s` in a SQL query and thinks \"SQL injection!\" — but `%s` with parameterized binding is actually the **fix** for SQL injection, not a vulnerability.\n\nIt's like a fire alarm going off every time you use the stove safely — annoying and useless if it can't tell the difference between cooking and an actual fire.\n\n**Why?** The training data had lots of \"SQL = dangerous\" examples but not enough \"parameterized SQL = safe\" examples, so the model learned \"any SQL with user input = bad.\"","thinking":"The user wants to understand what \"false positives on safe parameterized SQL (%s binding)\" means. Let me explain this simply."}
